{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-b919c66671f8b836", "type": "tool_result", "content": "1\tVisual Representation Matters: Exploiting Temporal Differences in Video-to-Audio Generation \n2\t\n3\tReport GitHub Issue\n4\t\n5\t × \n6\t\n7\t Title: \n8\t\n9\tContent selection saved. Describe the issue below:\n10\t\n11\t Description: \n12\t\n13\t Submit without GitHub \n14\t Submit in GitHub \n15\t\n16\t arXiv is now an independent nonprofit! \n17\t Learn more \n18\t × \n19\t\n20\t Back to arXiv \n21\t\n22\t Why HTML? \n23\t\n24\t Report Issue \n25\t\n26\t Back to Abstract \n27\t\n28\t Download PDF \n29\t\n30\t Abstract \n31\t\n32\t Introduction \n33\t\n34\t Related Work \n35\t\n36\t Temporal Differences. \n37\t\n38\t Visual Representation in V2A Generation. \n39\t\n40\t Preliminary \n41\t\n42\t Video-to-Audio Generation. \n43\t\n44\t Additional Supervision. \n45\t\n46\t Method \n47\t\n48\t Motivation: TD Distinguish V2A from I2A \n49\t\n50\t Investigation of TD Representation \n51\t\n52\t Frame-Level Temporal Differences (FTD). \n53\t\n54\t CLIP-Level Temporal Differences (CTD). \n55\t\n56\t TD Encoder. \n57\t\n58\t Hierarchically Continual Learning \n59\t\n60\t Textual Semantics. \n61\t\n62\t Visual Content. \n63\t\n64\t Temporal Differences. \n65\t\n66\t Annealed Temporal Differences Guidance \n67\t\n68\t Experiment \n69\t\n70\t Experimental Setup \n71\t\n72\t Datasets. \n73\t\n74\t Model Training. \n75\t\n76\t Model Inference. \n77\t\n78\t Evaluation Metrics. \n79\t\n80\t Main Results \n81\t\n82\t Ablation Studies \n83\t\n84\t Comparison with Different Conditions. \n85\t\n86\t Effectiveness of HCL Strategy. \n87\t\n88\t Impact of ATDG. \n89\t\n90\t Window Length. \n91\t\n92\t Conclusion \n93\t\n94\t References \n95\t\n96\t A Audio Latent Diffusion Model \n97\t\n98\t B Impact of Frame-Level Guidance Scale \n99\t\n100\t C Datasets \n101\t\n102\t D Compression Network \n103\t\n104\t E Model Configurations \n105\t\n106\t F Baseline Methods \n107\t\n108\t G Evaluation Metrics \n109\t\n110\t Objective Metrics \n111\t\n112\t Subjective Metrics \n113\t\n114\t H Comparison with VT2A methods \n115\t\n116\t I More Generation Results \n117\t\n118\t License: CC BY 4.0\n119\t\n120\tarXiv:2608.04902v1 [cs.CV] 05 Aug 2026\n121\t\n122\tVisual Representation Matters: Exploiting Temporal Differences in Video-to-Audio Generation\n123\t\n124\tZehua Chen 1 , Junyou Wang 1 1 1 footnotemark: 1 , Yuxuan Jiang 1 , Zhenying Fang 2 ,\n125\t Yusheng Dai 3 , Jianfei Chen 1 , Ziwei Liu 4 , Jun Zhu 1 \n126\t Equal contribution. E-mails: zhc23thuml@tsinghua.edu.cn, ackokomi0222@gmail.com.Corresponding author. E-mail: dcszj@tsinghua.edu.cn. \n127\t\n128\tAbstract\n129\t\n130\tVideo-to-audio (V2A) generation extends image-to-audio generation (I2A) by introducing consecutive frames that provide essential temporal cues for audio synthesis.\n131\tHowever, existing conditional diffusion-based V2A methods typically enhance visual conditioning with additional audio-visual supervision, acoustic structure prediction, or reasoning from large multimodal models, requiring extra networks or strong inductive biases.\n132\tInspired by recent advances in visual representation learning, we introduce TD-V2A, which leverages temporal differences (TD) as the key representation that distinguishes V2A from I2A, enriching visual conditioning with minimal architectural modification.\n133\tWe first investigate TD at both the frame and feature levels to identify the most effective representation level at which TD complements visual representations.\n134\tBased on these findings, we develop a hierarchically continual learning strategy and an annealed temporal differences guidance method to progressively learn and exploit TD information during diffusion training and sampling process, respectively.\n135\tExtensive experiments on benchmark datasets demonstrate that effectively exploiting TD through our proposed framework significantly improves end-to-end V2A generation quality, even outperforming dedicated V2A representations such as contrastive audio-visual pretraining.\n136\t\n137\tIntroduction\n138\t\n139\tGenerating audio from visual information has attracted increasing attention for its potential in augmented reality, film production, and multimodal content creation  (Zhao et al. 2018 ; Luo et al. 2023 ; Dai et al. 2026 ) . Early studies primarily focused on image-to-audio (I2A) generation  (Iashin and Rahtu 2021 ; Guzhov et al. 2022 ; Sheffer and Adi 2023 ) , where audio is synthesized from a single static image.\n140\tMore recently, research has progressed toward video-to-audio (V2A) generation, which aims to generate realistic and coherent audio for dynamic video inputs. Unlike I2A, V2A requires not only semantic consistency between visual content and generated audio but also temporal alignment between evolving visual events and corresponding sounds  (Luo et al. 2023 ; Wang et al. 2024b ; Cheng et al. 2025 ; Tian et al. 2026 ; Liu et al. 2026 ) .\n141\tThis additional challenge stems from the temporal differences (TD) between consecutive video frames, which characterize scene evolution over time.\n142\tWithout TD, a video reduces to a static image, and V2A inevitably collapses to an I2A setting  (Wang et al. 2025 ; Daithankar et al. 2026 ) .\n143\t\n144\tModern V2A systems are predominantly built upon conditional latent diffusion models, where the quality of the conditioning largely determines the fidelity of generated audio.\n145\tExisting methods therefore focus on enriching the conditioning information through three main strategies: introducing auxiliary audio-oriented supervision, such as coarse mel-spectrograms or temporal audio structures  (Wang et al. 2024b ; Jeong et al. 2025 ; Comunità et al. 2024 ; Ren et al. 2025 ) ; incorporating separately pre-trained audio-visual representation models  (Luo et al. 2023 ; Iashin et al. 2024 ) ; or leveraging multi-modal large language models for high-level video understanding  (Liu et al. 2026 ; Dai et al. 2026 ) . Although effective, these approaches rely on additional supervision, specialized pre-trained models, or strong inductive biases beyond the input video itself.\n146\tIn this work, inspired by recent advances in TD-based visual representation learning  (Daithankar et al. 2026 ) , we instead exploit the inherent TD between video frames to enhance visual representations. As the key factor distinguishing V2A from I2A, TD provides an intrinsic cue to enable superior V2A quality without introducing additional predictive networks or external conditioning signals.\n147\t\n148\t Figure 1: Overview of TD-V2A. Left: The framework exploits temporal differences (TD) for enriching visual representation to enhance video-to-audio generation. Right: A hierarchically continual learning strategy progressively learns textual semantics, visual content and TD, while annealed temporal differences guidance further exploits TD by dynamically adjusting its guidance strength across sampling steps.\n149\t\n150\tMotivated by this, we propose TD-V2A, which directly exploits TD representations derived from the input video without introducing additional supervision or dedicated extraction networks.\n151\tSince TD can be computed in diverse representation spaces, we begin with an investigation of its form at two levels: frame-level TD and feature-level TD.\n152\tFrame-level TD is obtained by differencing consecutive video frames before visual encoding, preserving complete visual variation while retaining the original visual input structure. This makes TD naturally compatible with pretrained visual encoders, allowing temporal cues to be incorporated with minimal architectural modification and inductive bias.\n153\tIn contrast, feature-level TD is computed from the differences between encoded frame representations, where temporal variation is measured in a compressed semantic space.\n154\tAlthough such representations capture high-level semantic transitions, the encoding process inevitably compresses and abstracts the original visual information, potentially discarding fine-grained temporal variations.\n155\tOur investigation demonstrates that preserving TD before visual encoding provides substantially more informative conditioning for V2A generation than modeling TD in the compressed feature space.\n156\t\n157\tWhen exploiting TD, the hierarchical structure of visual representations naturally motivates a progressive learning paradigm, where TD representations are progressively incorporated  (Jiang et al. 2026 ; Dai et al. 2026 ) .\n158\tTherefore, at the model training stage, we develop a Hierarchically Continual Learning (HCL) strategy for the diffusion models developed in a latent space compressed from waveform.\n159\tSpecifically, HCL gradually exploits increasingly informative visual representations through three training stages.\n160\tThe first stage initializes the model with large-scale text-audio pre-training to acquire high-quality audio priors that facilitate subsequent V2A learning.\n161\tThe second stage adapts the model to V2A generation using raw visual representations.\n162\tFinally, the third stage jointly optimizes the model under both raw visual conditioning and TD-augmented conditioning, enabling the conditional diffusion model to explicitly distinguish and effectively exploit TD for V2A generation.\n163\t\n164\tAt inference time, the hierarchical visual conditioning introduced by TD further inspires an Annealed Temporal Differences Guidance (ATDG) strategy. Combined with the coarse-to-fine denoising trajectory of diffusion models, ATDG progressively adjusts the influence of TD throughout the sampling process. Specifically, stronger TD guidance is applied during the early sampling stages to encourage the establishment of globally consistent temporal dynamics. As sampling proceeds toward finer details, the guidance strength is gradually annealed, allowing the generation process to rely increasingly on raw visual conditioning. By aligning the contribution of TD with the intrinsic denoising trajectory, ATDG enables the model to exploit TD information more effectively, resulting in more temporally coherent V2A generation.\n165\t\n166\tFollowing previous works, we develop TD-V2A on the VGGSound and AudioSet benchmarks. Without additional supervision or strong inductive biases, we introduce TD as an effective means to enhance visual representations for end-to-end V2A generation. Equipped with the proposed HCL and ATDG strategies, TD-V2A achieves superior results with a commonly used DiT backbone  (Evans and others 2024 ; Dai et al. 2026 ) , validated by both objective and subjective evaluations. Notably, it even outperforms the additionally developed CAVP model  (Luo et al. 2023 ) , highlighting TD as a simple yet powerful alternative to strengthen V2A quality.\n167\t\n168\tRelated Work\n169\t\n170\tTemporal Differences.\n171\t\n172\tTD modeling is a fundamental learning principle that exploits temporal consistency to learn predictive representations from sequential data. Originally introduced in reinforcement learning through bootstrapping  (Sutton 1988 ) , TD enables models to refine current estimates using future predictions without requiring complete future supervision.\n173\tOwing to its generality, the TD principle has been successfully extended beyond reinforcement learning to generative modeling  (Farebrother et al. 2025 ) and visual representation learning,  i.e. , TDV  (Daithankar et al. 2026 ) , where temporal transitions provide intrinsic supervisory signals for learning meaningful features.\n174\tThese developments demonstrate that TDs encode rich structural information about the underlying dynamics, making TD a powerful framework for exploiting sequential observations.\n175\tInspired by this principle, our TD-V2A exploits temporal variations between consecutive video frames as informative cues to enhance visual representation and consequently enhance V2A generation results.\n176\t\n177\tVisual Representation in V2A Generation.\n178\t\n179\tExisting V2A generation methods typically improve generation quality by introducing additional supervision or strong inductive biases beyond raw video inputs, such as separately trained audio-visual representation models  (Luo et al. 2023 ; Iashin et al. 2024 ) , auxiliary networks that predict intermediate audio structures (e.g., coarse mel-spectrograms  (Wang et al. 2024b ) or temporal energy patterns  (Jeong et al. 2025 ; Comunità et al. 2024 ; Ren et al. 2025 ) ), or large language models for high-level video understanding  (Liu et al. 2026 ; Dai et al. 2026 ) .\n180\tWhile effective, these approaches require specialized models or handcrafted conditioning signals.\n181\tIn contrast, our method directly exploits natural temporal differences inherent in the input video, using frame-level temporal variations as intrinsic cues to enhance video understanding without introducing additional predictive networks or external conditions, thereby reducing reliance on strong inductive biases while achieving superior V2A generation.\n182\t\n183\tPreliminary\n184\t\n185\tVideo-to-Audio Generation.\n186\t\n187\tV2A generation aims to synthesize audio 𝒙 0 \\bm{x}_{0} that is both semantically and temporally aligned with a given silent video input 𝒗 \\bm{v}   (Luo et al. 2023 ) .\n188\tRecent V2A methods  (Luo et al. 2023 ; Jeong et al. 2025 ; Cheng et al. 2025 ; Tian et al. 2026 ; Dai et al. 2026 ) typically adopt a conditional diffusion framework  (Liu et al. 2023 ; Evans and others 2024 ) , where audio is generated by progressively denoising a latent representation under the guidance of a video condition 𝒄 video ​ ( 𝒗 ) \\bm{c}_{\\text{video}}(\\bm{v}) extracted from the input video.\n189\tDuring training, the model learns the correspondence between video and audio, and during inference, the extracted video condition controls the generation process toward aligned audio outputs.\n190\tFormally, the diffusion training objective is defined as follows.\n191\t\n192\t ℒ v2a = 𝔼 𝒛 t , ϵ , 𝒄 video ​ [ ‖ ϵ − ϵ θ ​ ( 𝒛 t , t , 𝒄 video ​ ( 𝒗 ) ) ‖ 2 2 ] , \\mathcal{L}_{\\text{v2a}}=\\mathbb{E}_{\\bm{z}_{t},\\bm{\\epsilon},\\bm{c}_{\\text{video}}}\\Big[\\big\\|\\bm{\\epsilon}-\\bm{\\epsilon}_{\\theta}(\\bm{z}_{t},t,\\bm{c}_{\\text{video}}(\\bm{v}))\\big\\|_{2}^{2}\\Big], \n193\t\n194\t (1) \n195\t\n196\twhere 𝒛 t \\bm{z}_{t} is the noisy representation at time step t t constructed with the diffusion forward process in a small latent space 𝒛 \\bm{z} compressed from the audio space 𝒙 \\bm{x} .\n197\tAs shown, given this formulation, the quality of the generated audio naturally depends on how effectively the video condition captures the underlying visual content and temporal dynamics. Therefore, improving video representation is a critical factor for achieving high-quality V2A generation.\n198\t\n199\tAdditional Supervision.\n200\t\n201\tExisting V2A methods commonly enhance generation quality by introducing auxiliary supervision beyond the original video condition. Representative approaches employ separately trained audio-visual representation models for enriched cross-modal conditioning, predict intermediate audio representations (e.g., mel-spectrograms or temporal energy patterns) with auxiliary networks, or leverage large language models for high-level video understanding and reasoning. Despite their different implementations, these methods share a common principle: they introduce additional modules to construct a strong auxiliary condition 𝒄 aux ​ ( 𝒗 ) \\bm{c}_{\\text{aux}}(\\bm{v}) , which complements the original video representation during audio generation. The resulting diffusion objective can be generally formulated as follows.\n202\t\n203\t ℒ aux-v2a = 𝔼 𝒛 t , ϵ , 𝒄 video , 𝒄 aux ​ [ ‖ ϵ − ϵ θ ​ ( 𝒛 t , t , 𝒄 video ​ ( 𝒗 ) , 𝒄 aux ​ ( 𝒗 ) ) ‖ 2 2 ] . \\mathcal{L}_{\\text{aux-v2a}}=\\mathbb{E}_{\\bm{z}_{t},\\bm{\\epsilon},\\bm{c}_{\\text{video}},\\bm{c}_{\\text{aux}}}\\Big[\\big\\|\\bm{\\epsilon}-\\bm{\\epsilon}_{\\theta}(\\bm{z}_{t},t,\\bm{c}_{\\text{video}}(\\bm{v}),\\bm{c}_{\\text{aux}}(\\bm{v}))\\big\\|_{2}^{2}\\Big]. \n204\t\n205\t (2) \n206\t\n207\tWhile effective, these approaches rely on additional predictive models, handcrafted supervision, or external reasoning modules, leading to increased engineering complexity and stronger inductive biases.\n208\tIn contrast, we improve the quality of the original video representation itself through enhanced video representation learning. By strengthening visual representation directly from the input video, our method improves V2A without introducing additional auxiliary networks.\n209\t\n210\tMethod\n211\t\n212\tMotivation: TD Distinguish V2A from I2A\n213\t\n214\tExisting V2A methods primarily improve video-audio correspondence by introducing additional supervision or auxiliary modules with stronger inductive biases.\n215\tIn contrast, our goal is to enhance V2A generation from a more fundamental perspective: strengthening the video representation itself.\n216\tWe argue that the key lies in explicitly modeling TD, which constitute the essential distinction between V2A and I2A generation.  If a video contains no TD across frames, it effectively degenerates into a static image  (Wang et al. 2025 ; Daithankar et al. 2026 ) , reducing V2A to an I2A problem where temporal alignment is no longer a meaningful objective. \n217\tThis observation suggests that TD are not merely complementary cues, but the intrinsic visual information capturing temporal dynamics.\n218\tMotivated by this insight, we explicitly exploit TD from the input video to enable stronger visual representations with weaker inductive bias.\n219\tBy improving the quality of the original video representation rather than introducing additional auxiliary conditions, we achieve more temporally coherent and semantically faithful V2A generation in a simple end-to-end framework.\n220\t\n221\tInvestigation of TD Representation\n222\t\n223\t Figure 2: Illustration of TD modeling at different granularities. While CLIP-level TD (middle) captures high-level semantic variations between embeddings, frame-level TD (right) explicitly characterizes temporal dynamics by computing pixel-wise residuals between raw frames.\n224\t\n225\tWe begin by exploring TD representations at different levels of visual abstraction. Specifically, TD can be constructed either directly from consecutive video frames (frame-level TD) or from their corresponding visual features (feature-level TD).\n226\tSince CLIP embeddings are the de facto visual representation in modern V2A systems  (Wang et al. 2024b ; Cheng et al. 2025 ; Tian et al. 2026 ; Dai et al. 2026 ) , we instantiate feature-level TD as differences between consecutive CLIP embeddings, introducing temporal information with minimal architectural modification and without imposing additional inductive biases. In the following, we present the formulations of frame-level TD (FTD) and CLIP-level TD (CTD), respectively.\n227\t\n228\tFrame-Level Temporal Differences (FTD).\n229\t\n230\tAt the frame level, TD can be obtained by computing differences between consecutive raw frames.\n231\tFormally, given a sequence of video frames { 𝒗 n } n = 1 N \\{\\bm{v}^{n}\\}_{n=1}^{N} , the FTD computation is defined as:\n232\t\n233\t Δ ​ 𝒗 n = 𝒗 n + k − 𝒗 n , 𝒄 video TD ​ ( 𝒗 ) = [ 𝒉 ​ ( 𝒗 n ) ; 𝒉 ​ ( Δ ​ 𝒗 n ) ] , \\displaystyle\\Delta\\bm{v}^{n}=\\bm{v}^{n+k}-\\bm{v}^{n},\\quad\\bm{c}_{\\text{video}}^{\\text{TD}}(\\bm{v})=[\\bm{h}(\\bm{v}^{n});\\bm{h}(\\Delta\\bm{v}^{n})], \n234\t\n235\t (3) \n236\t\n237\twhere n = 1 , 2 , … , N − k n=1,2,\\dots,N-k ; k k is a hyperparameter with a small value controlling the window length when computing frame differences; and 𝒄 video TD \\bm{c}_{\\text{video}}^{\\text{TD}} is the computed video condition that incorporates TD.\n238\tConsidering each element in Δ ​ 𝒗 n \\Delta\\bm{v}^{n} , its amplitude reflects the magnitude of local temporal variations between consecutive frames. Different from raw frames that are dominated by static appearance information, FTD naturally suppresses temporally invariant components and highlights dynamic changes  (Daithankar et al. 2026 ) .\n239\tSince adjacent video frames usually exhibit strong temporal consistency, the resulting frame differences form a compact representation of visual evolution, where redundant background information is largely removed while informative motion patterns are preserved. Therefore, FTD provides a complementary visual representation that augments appearance features with explicit temporal variation cues, enabling V2A models to better capture the dynamic characteristics inherent in video inputs.\n240\t\n241\tTo enrich visual representations with explicit temporal variations without imposing strong inductive biases, we directly encode both raw frames 𝒗 n \\bm{v}^{n} and and their temporal differences Δ ​ 𝒗 n \\Delta\\bm{v}^{n} using a shared pretrained CLIP encoder 𝒉 \\bm{h} .\n242\tSince temporal differences remain visual observations that describe the evolution of scene content, they can be naturally embedded into the same representation space as raw frames, eliminating the need for dedicated motion encoders or additional architectural modifications.\n243\tFollowing previous works  (Tian et al. 2026 ; Dai et al. 2026 ) , the CLIP encoder is kept frozen during diffusion model training, allowing TD to be seamlessly incorporated into existing V2A frameworks while preserving their original visual backbone.\n244\t\n245\tCLIP-Level Temporal Differences (CTD).\n246\t\n247\tBeyond differences between raw frames, temporal variation can also be characterized directly in the CLIP embedding space.\n248\tSpecifically, given the CLIP representations { 𝒉 ​ ( 𝒗 n ) } n = 1 N \\{\\bm{h}(\\bm{v}^{n})\\}_{n=1}^{N} of video frames { 𝒗 n } n = 1 N \\{\\bm{v}^{n}\\}_{n=1}^{N} , we compute their temporal differences at the CLIP level as:\n249\t\n250\t Δ ​ 𝒉 ​ ( 𝒗 n ) = 𝒉 ​ ( 𝒗 n + k ) − 𝒉 ​ ( 𝒗 n ) , 𝒄 video TD ​ ( 𝒗 ) = [ 𝒉 ​ ( 𝒗 n ) ; Δ ​ 𝒉 ​ ( 𝒗 n ) ] , \\Delta\\bm{h}(\\bm{v}^{n})=\\bm{h}(\\bm{v}^{n+k})-\\bm{h}(\\bm{v}^{n}),\\bm{c}_{\\text{video}}^{\\text{TD}}(\\bm{v})=[\\bm{h}(\\bm{v}^{n});\\Delta\\bm{h}(\\bm{v}^{n})], \n251\t\n252\t (4) \n253\t\n254\twhere n = 1 , 2 , … , N − k n=1,2,\\dots,N-k ; k k again denotes the temporal window length for computing the difference in the CLIP embedding space.\n255\tCompared to FTD Δ ​ 𝒗 n \\Delta\\bm{v}^{n} , CTD Δ ​ 𝒉 ​ ( 𝒗 n ) \\Delta\\bm{h}(\\bm{v}^{n}) are computed after visual information has been projected into a high-level semantic representation. Consequently, they primarily capture semantic evolution while discarding a substantial amount of low-level visual variation. Although this abstraction improves semantic consistency, it may also attenuate fine-grained temporal changes that are preserved in FTD. Therefore, CTD provide a complementary view of temporal variation, emphasizing semantic transitions rather than detailed visual dynamics.\n256\t\n257\tTD Encoder.\n258\t\n259\tWe encode both raw frames and TD using the same pretrained CLIP encoder, treating TD as a complementary visual representation rather than introducing additional TD-specific encoders.\n260\tAlthough it is possible to develop dedicated encoders for TD or motion representations, doing so inevitably introduces additional architectural complexity and stronger task-specific inductive biases.\n261\tThis design is supported by CLIP’s strong visual generalization capability  (Radford et al. 2021 ) . For example, CLIP achieves robust recognition on ImageNet-Sketch  (Wang et al. 2019 ) , indicating that its representations remain effective even when visual appearance is largely reduced to contours and structural cues  (Radford et al. 2021 ) . Since FTD similarly suppress static appearance while emphasizing structural changes, they can be naturally encoded within the same visual representation space. We therefore retain the standard frozen CLIP encoder throughout, enabling a controlled evaluation of TD under minimal architectural modification and inductive bias.\n262\t\n263\tHierarchically Continual Learning\n264\t\n265\tRaw frames and TD provide hierarchical visual representations from appearance to temporal variation, naturally motivating a progressive learning strategy to fully exploit visual information. Based on this observation, we develop a three-stage hierarchical training framework that incrementally incorporates more detailed visual representations.\n266\t\n267\tTextual Semantics.\n268\t\n269\tThe T2A pre-training stage provides a strong initialization for V2A, allowing the model to acquire advanced audio generation capabilities before introducing visual conditioning. In this stage, we adopt Stable Audio  (Evans et al. 2024 ) as the T2A backbone, which employs a DiT-based latent diffusion architecture operating on a compressed audio representation derived directly from waveform signals. Its powerful audio modeling capability provides a strong foundation for subsequent video-conditioned audio generation.\n270\tFormally, the T2A LDM is pretrained to generate a latent audio representation 𝒛 0 \\bm{z}_{0} conditioned on a text embedding 𝒄 text \\bm{c}_{\\text{text}} , with the objective:\n271\t\n272\t ℒ Stage1-T2A = 𝔼 𝒛 0 , ϵ , t ​ [ ‖ ϵ − ϵ θ ​ ( 𝒛 t , t , 𝒄 text ) ‖ 2 2 ] , \\mathcal{L}_{\\text{Stage1-T2A}}=\\mathbb{E}_{\\bm{z}_{0},\\bm{\\epsilon},t}\\Big[\\big\\|\\bm{\\epsilon}-\\bm{\\epsilon}_{\\theta}(\\bm{z}_{t},t,\\bm{c}_{\\text{text}})\\big\\|_{2}^{2}\\Big], \n273\t\n274\t (5) \n275\t\n276\twhere 𝒄 text \\bm{c}_{\\text{text}} can be extracted with either contrastive pre-training models,  e.g. , CLAP  (Wu et al. 2023 ) , or language models,  e.g. , FLAN-T5  (Chung et al. 2024 ) .\n277\t\n278\tVisual Content.\n279\t\n280\tStarting from the pretrained T2A model, we further adapt it to V2A generation by replacing the text condition with visual representations. Since T2A and V2A share the same diffusion framework  (Tian et al. 2026 ; Dai et al. 2026 ) , the pretrained audio generation capability can be naturally transferred to video-conditioned generation.\n281\tFormally, the fine-tuning objective replaces the text embedding 𝒄 text \\bm{c}_{\\text{text}} with a visual embedding,  e.g. , CLIP embeddings  (Radford et al. 2021 ) , extracted from raw video frames:\n282\t\n283\t ℒ Stage2-V2A = 𝔼 𝒛 0 , ϵ , t ​ [ ‖ ϵ − ϵ θ ​ ( 𝒛 t , t , 𝒄 video ) ‖ 2 2 ] . \\mathcal{L}_{\\text{Stage2-V2A}}=\\mathbb{E}_{\\bm{z}_{0},\\bm{\\epsilon},t}\\Big[\\big\\|\\bm{\\epsilon}-\\bm{\\epsilon}_{\\theta}(\\bm{z}_{t},t,\\bm{c}_{\\text{video}})\\big\\|_{2}^{2}\\Big]. \n284\t\n285\t (6) \n286\t\n287\tFollowing recent V2A and VT2A generation systems  (Wang et al. 2024b ; Cheng et al. 2025 ; Tian et al. 2026 ; Dai et al. 2026 ) , we use CLIP  (Radford et al. 2021 ) to extract visual embeddings from raw video frames as the conditioning signal. The visual-conditioned fine-tuning enables the model to establish video-audio correspondence while retaining the audio generation prior learned during T2A pre-training.\n288\t\n289\tTemporal Differences.\n290\t\n291\tAfter obtaining the visual-conditioned V2A model, we further introduce TD to enrich the visual representation. Rather than replacing raw frame features, TD serves as a complementary representation that explicitly characterizes frame-to-frame changes while remaining derived from the same visual observations.\n292\tSince the model has already learned the basic visual representation through V2A fine-tuning, TD is introduced only in the third stage. Specifically, we jointly optimize both the original V2A task and the TD-enhanced V2A task, allowing the model to progressively learn the contribution of TD while preserving its original visual representation capability. The TD-enhanced objective is formulated as follows.\n293\t\n294\t ℒ Stage3-TDV2A = 𝔼 𝒛 0 , ϵ , t ​ [ ‖ ϵ − ϵ θ ​ ( 𝒛 t , t , 𝒄 video TD ) ‖ 2 2 ] . \\mathcal{L}_{\\text{Stage3-TDV2A}}=\\mathbb{E}_{\\bm{z}_{0},\\bm{\\epsilon},t}\\Big[\\big\\|\\bm{\\epsilon}-\\bm{\\epsilon}_{\\theta}(\\bm{z}_{t},t,\\bm{c}_{\\text{video}}^{\\text{TD}})\\big\\|_{2}^{2}\\Big]. \n295\t\n296\t (7) \n297\t\n298\twhere 𝒄 video TD \\bm{c}_{\\text{video}}^{\\text{TD}} denotes the video condition that integrates TD at frame or CLIP level, computed as Equation ( 3 ) or Equation ( 4 ) defined above.\n299\t\n300\t Method \n301\t\n302\t Pre-training \n303\t\n304\t Condition \n305\t\n306\t Guidance \n307\t\n308\t Audio Quality \n309\t\n310\t Semantic \n311\t\n312\t Temporal \n313\t\n314\t FAD ↓ \\downarrow \n315\t\n316\t KL ↓ \\downarrow \n317\t\n318\t IS ↑ \\uparrow \n319\t\n320\t FD ↓ \\downarrow \n321\t\n322\t IBS ↑ \\uparrow \n323\t\n324\t AA ↑ \\uparrow \n325\t\n326\tGT\n327\t\n328\t-\n329\t\n330\t-\n331\t\n332\t-\n333\t\n334\t-\n335\t\n336\t-\n337\t\n338\t-\n339\t\n340\t-\n341\t\n342\t32.9\n343\t\n344\t83.6\n345\t\n346\tIM2WAV\n347\t\n348\t✗\n349\t\n350\tCLIP\n351\t\n352\tCFG\n353\t\n354\t6.41\n355\t\n356\t2.54\n357\t\n358\t-\n359\t\n360\t-\n361\t\n362\t19.0\n363\t\n364\t74.3\n365\t\n366\tDiff-Foley\n367\t\n368\tT2I\n369\t\n370\tCAVP † \n371\t\n372\tCFG+CG\n373\t\n374\t5.79\n375\t\n376\t3.12\n377\t\n378\t10.8\n379\t\n380\t21.90\n381\t\n382\t20.4\n383\t\n384\t 89.9 \n385\t\n386\tFoleyGen\n387\t\n388\t✗\n389\t\n390\tCLIP\n391\t\n392\tCFG\n393\t\n394\t1.65\n395\t\n396\t2.35\n397\t\n398\t-\n399\t\n400\t-\n401\t\n402\t26.1\n403\t\n404\t73.8\n405\t\n406\tVTA-LDM\n407\t\n408\tUnlabeled V/A\n409\t\n410\tCLIP4CLIP\n411\t\n412\tCFG\n413\t\n414\t2.01\n415\t\n416\t2.37\n417\t\n418\t10.4\n419\t\n420\t12.80\n421\t\n422\t26.2\n423\t\n424\t77.0\n425\t\n426\tFoleyCrafter\n427\t\n428\tT2A\n429\t\n430\tCLIP+Timestamp † \n431\t\n432\t✗\n433\t\n434\t2.32\n435\t\n436\t2.54\n437\t\n438\t9.9\n439\t\n440\t18.10\n441\t\n442\t27.7\n443\t\n444\t83.6\n445\t\n446\tFrieren\n447\t\n448\t✗\n449\t\n450\tCAVP\n451\t\n452\tCFG\n453\t\n454\t1.23\n455\t\n456\t2.73\n457\t\n458\t11.3\n459\t\n460\t12.13\n461\t\n462\t21.0\n463\t\n464\t-\n465\t\n466\tV2A-Mapper\n467\t\n468\t✗ ∗ \n469\t\n470\tCLIP (CLAP) †∗∗ \n471\t\n472\tCFG\n473\t\n474\t0.90\n475\t\n476\t2.68\n477\t\n478\t 12.5 \n479\t\n480\t8.35\n481\t\n482\t22.4\n483\t\n484\t78.3\n485\t\n486\tVAB-Encodec\n487\t\n488\tV-A pairs\n489\t\n490\teva-CLIP\n491\t\n492\tCFG\n493\t\n494\t2.69\n495\t\n496\t2.58\n497\t\n498\t-\n499\t\n500\t-\n501\t\n502\t-\n503\t\n504\t-\n505\t\n506\tVATT ⋄ \n507\t\n508\tV-A pairs\n509\t\n510\tTrained visual feature † \n511\t\n512\tCFG\n513\t\n514\t2.35\n515\t\n516\t2.25\n517\t\n518\t-\n519\t\n520\t-\n521\t\n522\t-\n523\t\n524\t82.8\n525\t\n526\tMMAudio ⋄ \n527\t\n528\t✗\n529\t\n530\tCLIP+Syncformer † \n531\t\n532\tCFG\n533\t\n534\t 0.81 \n535\t\n536\t 2.11 \n537\t\n538\t11.9\n539\t\n540\t 5.65 \n541\t\n542\t 28.0 \n543\t\n544\t-\n545\t\n546\tAudioX ⋄ \n547\t\n548\tT2A\n549\t\n550\tCLIP+Syncformer † \n551\t\n552\tCFG\n553\t\n554\t1.13\n555\t\n556\t2.57\n557\t\n558\t12.2\n559\t\n560\t8.83\n561\t\n562\t26.0\n563\t\n564\t-\n565\t\n566\tTD-V2A (Ours)\n567\t\n568\tHCL\n569\t\n570\tCLIP+FTD\n571\t\n572\tATDG\n573\t\n574\t 0.53 \n575\t\n576\t 2.16 \n577\t\n578\t 16.9 \n579\t\n580\t 3.79 \n581\t\n582\t 33.8 \n583\t\n584\t 89.1 \n585\t\n586\t Table 1: Comparison of our method with baseline models on the VGGSound test set. The best performance for each metric is in bold, while the second-best is marked with an underline. In the \"Pre-training\" column, \"T\", \"I\", \"A\", \"V\" stands for text, image, audio and video, respectively. ⋄ : does not use text during inference. † : the feature requires additional network or training. ∗ : V2A-Mapper directly uses T2A (AudioLDM) for generation. ∗∗ : The mapper translating CLIP into CLAP needs training.\n587\t\n588\tAnnealed Temporal Differences Guidance\n589\t\n590\tDuring inference, diffusion models progressively reconstruct structured signals from Gaussian noise through a coarse-to-fine denoising process. In our framework, TD provide a complementary visual representation that captures frame-to-frame variations beyond the semantic information encoded by raw frame features. While HCL enhances the learning of TD representations during training, we further exploit these representations during inference through an adaptive guidance strategy.\n591\tSpecifically, the jointly optimized model provides two complementary conditional predictions: one conditioned on raw frame representations and the other on TD-enhanced representations. The difference between them reflects the contribution of TD representations, which serves as an explicit temporal guidance signal during sampling.\n592\tLet ϵ 𝜽 ​ ( 𝒛 t , t ) \\bm{\\epsilon}_{\\bm{\\theta}}(\\bm{z}_{t},t) denote the unconditional prediction, ϵ 𝜽 ​ ( 𝒛 t , t , 𝒄 video ) \\bm{\\epsilon}_{\\bm{\\theta}}(\\bm{z}_{t},t,\\bm{c}_{\\text{video}}) the prediction conditioned on frame-level features, and ϵ 𝜽 ​ ( 𝒛 t , t , 𝒄 video TD ) \\bm{\\epsilon}_{\\bm{\\theta}}(\\bm{z}_{t},t,\\bm{c}_{\\text{video}}^{\\text{TD}}) the prediction conditioned on both raw frame and temporal differences. The final guided prediction is computed as follows.\n593\t\n594\t ϵ 𝜽 ATDG \\displaystyle\\bm{\\epsilon}_{\\bm{\\theta}}^{\\text{ATDG}} \n595\t\n596\t = ϵ 𝜽 ​ ( 𝒛 t , t ) + w f ​ ( ϵ 𝜽 ​ ( 𝒛 t , t , 𝒄 video TD ) − ϵ 𝜽 ​ ( 𝒛 t , t ) ) \\displaystyle=\\bm{\\epsilon}_{\\bm{\\theta}}(\\bm{z}_{t},t)+w_{f}\\Big(\\bm{\\epsilon}_{\\bm{\\theta}}(\\bm{z}_{t},t,\\bm{c}_{\\text{video}}^{\\text{TD}})-\\bm{\\epsilon}_{\\bm{\\theta}}(\\bm{z}_{t},t)\\Big) \n597\t\n598\t (8) \n599\t\n600\t + w TD ​ ( t ) ⋅ ( ϵ 𝜽 ​ ( 𝒛 t , t , 𝒄 video TD ) − ϵ 𝜽 ​ ( 𝒛 t , t , 𝒄 video ) ) , \\displaystyle+w_{\\text{TD}}(t)\\cdot\\Big(\\bm{\\epsilon}_{\\bm{\\theta}}(\\bm{z}_{t},t,\\bm{c}_{\\text{video}}^{\\text{TD}})-\\bm{\\epsilon}_{\\bm{\\theta}}(\\bm{z}_{t},t,\\bm{c}_{\\text{video}})\\Big), \n601\t\n602\twhere w f w_{f} is a constant guidance scale for frame-level semantics, and w TD ​ ( t ) w_{\\text{TD}}(t) is a timestep-dependent weight for TD.\n603\tTo emphasize temporal guidance at early stages, we define:\n604\t\n605\t w TD ​ ( t ) = w TD min + ( w TD max − w TD min ) ⋅ ( t T ) γ , w_{\\text{TD}}(t)=w^{\\text{min}}_{\\text{TD}}+(w^{\\text{max}}_{\\text{TD}}-w^{\\text{min}}_{\\text{TD}})\\cdot(\\frac{t}{T})^{\\gamma}, \n606\t\n607\t (9) \n608\t\n609\twhere T T is the total number of sampling steps, t t decreases from T T to 0 , and γ \\gamma is the annealing factor.\n610\tUnder this formulation, TD dominate early steps and are gradually annealed, allowing semantic guidance to take over in later stages.\n611\tThis design follows the coarse-to-fine nature of diffusion sampling, improving temporal alignment while preserving semantic fidelity. It can be viewed as a mixture-of-guidance strategy  (Wang et al. 2026 ) , where variation and semantic cues contribute complementarily, yielding targeted alignment gains without additional inference cost, as demonstrated by our experimental results.\n612\t\n613\tExperiment\n614\t\n615\tExperimental Setup\n616\t\n617\tDatasets.\n618\t\n619\tFor T2A pre-training, we utilize AudioCaps training set (Kim et al. 2019 ) , AudioSet (Gemmeke et al. 2017 ) , VGGSound training set (Chen et al. 2020 ) , FreeSound (Font et al. 2013 ) , and MSD (Bertin-Mahieux et al. 2011 ) . For visual and temporal differences fine-tuning, we use AudioSet and VGGSound training sets (detailed in Appendix  C ). All audio tracks are segmented into 10-second clips and resampled to 16 kHz.\n620\tFollowing previous works  (Luo et al. 2023 ; Tian et al. 2026 ; Zhang et al. 2026 ) , we evaluate on the VGGSound test set, consisting of about 15K 10-second audio clips.\n621\t\n622\t Method \n623\t\n624\t OVL ↑ \\uparrow \n625\t\n626\t S-REL ↑ \\uparrow \n627\t\n628\t T-REL ↑ \\uparrow \n629\t\n630\tGT\n631\t\n632\t 3.81 ± 0.49 3.81\\pm 0.49 \n633\t\n634\t 3.92 ± 0.55 3.92\\pm 0.55 \n635\t\n636\t 3.72 ± 0.47 3.72\\pm 0.47 \n637\t\n638\tDiff-Foley\n639\t\n640\t 2.46 ± 0.49 2.46\\pm 0.49 \n641\t\n642\t 2.59 ± 0.54 2.59\\pm 0.54 \n643\t\n644\t 2.21 ± 0.45 2.21\\pm 0.45 \n645\t\n646\tFoleyCrafter\n647\t\n648\t 3.17 ± 0.47 3.17\\pm 0.47 \n649\t\n650\t 3.23 ± 0.56 3.23\\pm 0.56 \n651\t\n652\t 3.04 ± 0.50 3.04\\pm 0.50 \n653\t\n654\tTD-V2A (Ours)\n655\t\n656\t 3.68 ± 0.46 \\bm{3.68\\pm 0.46} \n657\t\n658\t 3.85 ± 0.51 \\bm{3.85\\pm 0.51} \n659\t\n660\t 3.62 ± 0.48 \\bm{3.62\\pm 0.48} \n661\t\n662\t Table 2: Subjective evaluation results for different methods.\n663\t\n664\tModel Training.\n665\t\n666\tDuring T2A pre-training, we use FLAN-T5  (Chung et al. 2024 ) as the text encoder, as it provides a stronger initialization for subsequent fine-tuning than CLIP or CLAP. We first train a Variational Autoencoder (VAE) to compress waveforms into latent representations, and pretrain the T2A model for 2M iterations with a total batch size of 64. For visual and temporal differences fine-tuning, we employ the same VAE and Diffusion Transformer (DiT) (Peebles and Xie 2023 ) backbone as in T2A, and each for 0.3M iterations with the same total batch size on 8 GPUs. We use the AdamW optimizer with a learning rate of 5 × 10 − 5 5\\times 10^{-5} . Implementation details are further provided in Appendix  D and Appendix  E .\n667\t\n668\tModel Inference.\n669\t\n670\tTo apply ATDG, we train with a dropout probability of 0.1 on the CLIP condition 𝒄 video \\bm{c}_{\\text{video}} during visual fine-tuning. In the final fine-tuning with temporal differences, we apply 0.05 dropout to the TD condition and another 0.05 dropout to both conditions. The reason for using smaller probabilities is that ϵ 𝜽 ​ ( 𝒛 t , t ) \\bm{\\epsilon}_{\\bm{\\theta}}(\\bm{z}_{t},t) and ϵ 𝜽 ​ ( 𝒛 t , t , 𝒄 video ) \\bm{\\epsilon}_{\\bm{\\theta}}(\\bm{z}_{t},t,\\bm{c}_{\\text{video}}) have already been trained in the previous visual fine-tuning stage.\n671\tWe set w f = 2.0 , w TD min = 0.5 , w TD max = 1.5 w_{f}=2.0,w^{\\text{min}}_{\\text{TD}}=0.5,w^{\\text{max}}_{\\text{TD}}=1.5 and γ = 1.2 \\gamma=1.2 in ATDG. During inference, we set the number of sampling steps to 67 for ATDG and 100 for pure CFG, ensuring the same number of function evaluations (NFE).\n672\t\n673\tEvaluation Metrics.\n674\t\n675\tWe evaluate our models and baselines on audio quality and video-audio alignment, both semantically and temporally. Objective metrics include commonly used Fréchet Audio Distance (FAD), Kullback-Leibler (KL) divergence, Inception Score (IS), Fréchet Distance (FD) (using the PANN tagging model), Imagebind Score (IBS) (Girdhar et al. 2023 ) and temporal alignment accuracy (AA) employed in Diff-Foley. For subjective evaluation, we recruit 15\n676\thuman raters to score three aspects: (i) overall audio quality (OVL), (ii) semantic relevance to the input video (S-REL) and (iii) temporal synchronization with the video (T-REL). All scores are rated on a 1–5 scale. More details of these metrics are introduced in Appendix  G .\n677\t\n678\t Figure 3: Performance comparison across dynamic and static scenes. In the dynamic instrument scene, TD-V2A achieves precise rhythmic synchronization with GT. For the static scene, TD-V2A maintains higher audio quality and semantic fidelity compared to other baselines. We put more samples in Appendix  I .\n679\t\n680\tMain Results\n681\t\n682\tWe compare our results with GT ( i.e. , ground truth) and a variety of V2A systems 1 1 1 Some recent methods, such as ThinkSound, utilize extra text information (VT2A) and are not directly comparable to standard V2A generation. We further compare with them in Appendix  H . , including IM2WAV (Sheffer and Adi 2023 ) , Diff-Foley (Luo et al. 2023 ) , FoleyGen (Mei et al. 2024 ) , VTA-LDM (Xu et al. 2024 ) , FoleyCrafter (Zhang et al. 2026 ) , Frieren (Wang et al. 2024c ) , V2A-Mapper (Wang et al. 2024a ) , VAB-Encodec (Su et al. 2024 ) , VATT (Liu et al. 2024b ) , MMAudio (Cheng et al. 2025 ) and AudioX (Tian et al. 2026 ) . Detailed descriptions of these methods are provided in Appendix  F .\n683\tThe quantitative results are summarized in Table 1 . Our method considering HCL, FTD and ATDG outperforms the baseline models, with especially remarkable improvements in the FAD, IS, and IBS metrics.\n684\tMore importantly, apart from Diff-Foley, which pretrains contrastive models with AA objective on VGGSound and AudioSet datasets to directly optimize this metric, our method surpasses all other baselines on AA and achieves performance comparable to Diff-Foley.\n685\tIn the following ablation studies, we also observe that compared with using condition signals from raw frames, both FTD and CTD achieve better generation quality, further validating the effectiveness of our methods.\n686\t\n687\t Pret. \n688\t\n689\t Condition \n690\t\n691\t FAD ↓ \\downarrow \n692\t\n693\t KL ↓ \\downarrow \n694\t\n695\t IS ↑ \\uparrow \n696\t\n697\t FD ↓ \\downarrow \n698\t\n699\t IBS ↑ \\uparrow \n700\t\n701\t AA ↑ \\uparrow \n702\t\n703\tT2A\n704\t\n705\tCLIP\n706\t\n707\t0.64\n708\t\n709\t2.20\n710\t\n711\t16.6\n712\t\n713\t4.33\n714\t\n715\t31.8\n716\t\n717\t87.2\n718\t\n719\tT2A\n720\t\n721\tCLIP+CAVP\n722\t\n723\t0.57\n724\t\n725\t 2.16 \n726\t\n727\t16.4\n728\t\n729\t4.06\n730\t\n731\t31.9\n732\t\n733\t88.5\n734\t\n735\tT2A\n736\t\n737\tCLIP+CTD\n738\t\n739\t0.57\n740\t\n741\t2.20\n742\t\n743\t 17.1 \n744\t\n745\t3.93\n746\t\n747\t33.3\n748\t\n749\t88.3\n750\t\n751\tT2A\n752\t\n753\tCLIP+FTD\n754\t\n755\t0.55\n756\t\n757\t2.18\n758\t\n759\t16.5\n760\t\n761\t3.98\n762\t\n763\t33.4\n764\t\n765\t88.8\n766\t\n767\tHCL\n768\t\n769\tCLIP+FTD\n770\t\n771\t 0.53 \n772\t\n773\t 2.16 \n774\t\n775\t16.9\n776\t\n777\t 3.79 \n778\t\n779\t 33.8 \n780\t\n781\t 89.1 \n782\t\n783\t Table 3: Ablation studies on pre-training (denoted as Pret.) and condition.\n784\t\n785\tAblation Studies\n786\t\n787\tComparison with Different Conditions.\n788\t\n789\tTo prove the effectiveness of our proposed auxiliary information for V2A, FTD and CTD, we compare their results with our baseline model with pure CLIP and CLIP+CAVP in Table  3 .\n790\tOur methods yield consistent improvements across all evaluation metrics over using only CLIP, with FTD exhibiting more substantial gains. Moreover, the FTD model even achieves better results than CLIP+CAVP, which requires extra training objectives and stages. These results further validate that incorporating TD information, through capturing visual dynamics, enhances the performance of V2A generation.\n791\t\n792\tEffectiveness of HCL Strategy.\n793\t\n794\tNext, we demonstrate the effectiveness of our HCL strategy by comparing it with pure T2A pre-training. As shown in Table  3 , T2A pre-training first provides a strong initialization, allowing the model to learn fundamental audio generation capabilities before incorporating video conditions. Building upon this foundation, our proposed HCL method progressively introduces TD features, enabling the model to develop more fine-grained capabilities while retaining the knowledge acquired in earlier stages. Consequently, HCL achieves further improvements over the already strong T2A-pretrained model.\n795\t\n796\t Setting \n797\t\n798\t FAD ↓ \\downarrow \n799\t\n800\t KL ↓ \\downarrow \n801\t\n802\t IS ↑ \\uparrow \n803\t\n804\t FD ↓ \\downarrow \n805\t\n806\t IBS ↑ \\uparrow \n807\t\n808\t AA ↑ \\uparrow \n809\t\n810\tLow static ( w TD = 0.5 w_{\\text{TD}}=0.5 )\n811\t\n812\t0.57\n813\t\n814\t2.18\n815\t\n816\t16.7\n817\t\n818\t3.85\n819\t\n820\t33.6\n821\t\n822\t88.2\n823\t\n824\tHigh static ( w TD = 1.5 w_{\\text{TD}}=1.5 )\n825\t\n826\t0.62\n827\t\n828\t2.21\n829\t\n830\t16.5\n831\t\n832\t4.01\n833\t\n834\t33.0\n835\t\n836\t 89.1 \n837\t\n838\tATDG (Ours)\n839\t\n840\t 0.53 \n841\t\n842\t 2.16 \n843\t\n844\t 16.9 \n845\t\n846\t 3.79 \n847\t\n848\t 33.8 \n849\t\n850\t 89.1 \n851\t\n852\t Table 4: Ablation of TD guidance scale w TD w_{\\text{TD}} .\n853\t\n854\t k k \n855\t\n856\t FAD ↓ \\downarrow \n857\t\n858\t KL ↓ \\downarrow \n859\t\n860\t IS ↑ \\uparrow \n861\t\n862\t FD ↓ \\downarrow \n863\t\n864\t IBS ↑ \\uparrow \n865\t\n866\t AA ↑ \\uparrow \n867\t\n868\t1\n869\t\n870\t0.58\n871\t\n872\t2.21\n873\t\n874\t 17.2 \n875\t\n876\t4.11\n877\t\n878\t33.4\n879\t\n880\t88.6\n881\t\n882\t2\n883\t\n884\t 0.53 \n885\t\n886\t 2.16 \n887\t\n888\t16.9\n889\t\n890\t 3.79 \n891\t\n892\t 33.8 \n893\t\n894\t 89.1 \n895\t\n896\t3\n897\t\n898\t0.59\n899\t\n900\t2.17\n901\t\n902\t16.9\n903\t\n904\t3.94\n905\t\n906\t 33.8 \n907\t\n908\t88.8\n909\t\n910\t Table 5: Ablation studies on window length k k .\n911\t\n912\tImpact of ATDG.\n913\t\n914\tWe further compare our proposed ATDG with two static w TD w_{\\text{TD}} settings in Table  4 . As shown, a small constant w TD w_{\\text{TD}} preserves the perceptual quality of the generated audio, but fails to provide sufficient visual difference conditioning, leading to worse temporal synchronization. In contrast, a large static w TD w_{\\text{TD}} introduces noticeable distortion and degrades audio quality. Our method, ATDG, effectively achieves a balance by emphasizing TD in the early stages and gradually reducing it in later steps. These results highlight the importance of dynamically annealing the guidance strength for effectively exploiting TD while maintaining high audio quality throughout the diffusion process. We also ablate the frame-level guidance scale w f w_{f} in Appendix  B .\n915\t\n916\tWindow Length.\n917\t\n918\tWe also investigate the effect of our hyperparameter, namely the window length k k , in the setting of CLIP+FTD. Specifically, we set k k to 1, 2, and 3, and report the results in Table  5 . We observe that when k = 1 k=1 , the window is too short to effectively capture visual dynamics, while when k = 3 k=3 , the window becomes too large and may be sensitive to distracting information in the video. In contrast, k = 2 k=2 serves as a balanced choice, as it not only captures short-term dynamics but also preserves fine-grained details in the video, thereby achieving the best performance.\n919\t\n920\tConclusion\n921\t\n922\tIn this work, we identify TD as the fundamental cue that distinguishes V2A from I2A generation. Building upon this insight, we show that explicitly exploiting TD provides a simple yet effective paradigm for V2A, enabling strong generation results without additional supervision, strong inductive biases, or substantial architectural modifications.\n923\tTogether with HCL and ATDG, TD consistently improves generation quality and even surpasses the additionally developed CAVP model, demonstrating the remarkable potential of explicit TD modeling for future V2A research.\n924\t\n925\tReferences\n926\t\n927\t T. Bertin-Mahieux, D. P. Ellis, B. Whitman, and P. Lamere (2011) \n928\t The million song dataset .\n929\t\n930\t In ISMIR ,\n931\t\n932\t pp. 591–596 .\n933\t\n934\t Cited by: Table 6 ,\n935\t Appendix C ,\n936\t Datasets. .\n937\t\n938\t H. Chen, W. Xie, A. Vedaldi, and A. Zisserman (2020) \n939\t VGGSound: a large-scale audio-visual dataset .\n940\t\n941\t In ICASSP ,\n942\t\n943\t pp. 721–725 .\n944\t\n945\t Cited by: Table 6 ,\n946\t Appendix C ,\n947\t Datasets. .\n948\t\n949\t H. K. Cheng, M. Ishii, A. Hayakawa, T. Shibuya, A. Schwing, and Y. Mitsufuji (2025) \n950\t MMAudio: taming multimodal joint training for high-quality video-to-audio synthesis .\n951\t\n952\t In CVPR ,\n953\t\n954\t pp. 28901–28911 .\n955\t\n956\t Cited by: Appendix F ,\n957\t Introduction ,\n958\t Video-to-Audio Generation. ,\n959\t Investigation of TD Representation ,\n960\t Visual Content. ,\n961\t Main Results .\n962\t\n963\t H. W. Chung, L. Hou, S. Longpre, B. Zoph, Y. Tay, W. Fedus, Y. Li, X. Wang, M. Dehghani, S. Brahma, et al. (2024) \n964\t Scaling instruction-finetuned language models .\n965\t\n966\t JMLR 25 ( 70 ), pp. 1–53 .\n967\t\n968\t Cited by: Appendix E ,\n969\t Textual Semantics. ,\n970\t Model Training. .\n971\t\n972\t M. Comunità, R. F. Gramaccioni, E. Postolache, E. Rodolà, D. Comminiello, a